llama: bound the recurrent rollback by the depth that the last ubatch wrote

Base: llama.cpp commit c6824a9 plus the patches before this one in patches/series.

Files:
    src/llama-memory-recurrent.h            the per-seq depth of the snapshot groups
    src/llama-memory-recurrent.cpp          the depth, and the limit that seq_rm applies
    src/llama-kv-cache-dsv4.h               the same depth of the DeepSeek V4 cache
    src/llama-kv-cache-dsv4.cpp             the depth, and the limit that its seq_rm applies
    tests/test-recurrent-state-rollback.cpp the rule, and the owners of the contexts

The problem

A context with n_rs_seq > 0 keeps K = n_rs_seq + 1 groups of the recurrent state of each
sequence. A partial llama_memory_seq_rm of r tokens sets rs_idx to r, and the next graph reads
group r through s_copy.

The graph of a ubatch that holds T tokens of one sequence writes the groups 0 thru
min(T, K) - 1, and group g holds the state after the token T - g of that ubatch. Refer to
llm_build_delta_net_base::build_recurrent_attn, where n_written is min(n_seq_tokens, K). Thus
each group from min(T, K) upward keeps what an earlier ubatch left in it, and that state belongs
to a different position.

seq_rm does a check of r against n_rs_seq only. It has no value for T. A caller that asks for a
rollback deeper than the last ubatch gets the state of a different position, no error message,
and wrong logits. The condition is not documented and no assert states it.

The measurement

A host program decodes a prefill of 64 tokens on two contexts. The roll context decodes a tail of
T more tokens and removes all T of them again, thus a correct rollback puts it in the state of
the reference context. Both contexts then decode one identical batch of 8 tokens, thus the ubatch
shapes agree and a correct result matches bitwise. The model is Qwen3.5-2B Q4_0 on the CPU
backend. The floor of a comparison of two different batch shapes is 0.168 on the same model.

    case                    max|dlogit| before   after
    tail T=1 r=1            2.20884              refused
    tail T=2 r=2            2.18867              refused
    tail T=3 r=3            2.18571              refused
    tail T=4 r=4  (K=9)     1.98843              refused
    tail T=8 r=8  (K=9)     2.23574              refused
    inner T=4 r=1           0                    0
    inner T=4 r=2           0                    0
    inner T=4 r=3           0.16409              0.16409

Every rollback that undoes a whole ubatch reads a stale group today. Every rollback that lands
inside its ubatch is correct, and this patch does not change it.

The change

llama_memory_recurrent keeps one more value for each sequence, rs_depth, which is the deepest
rollback that the groups of that sequence hold. The memory context sets it in apply(), one time
for each ubatch, from the token count of the ubatch. seq_rm takes that value as its limit in
place of n_rs_seq, thus a rollback that reaches a group which no ubatch wrote returns false.

The value goes to zero when the sequence is cleared, when the whole sequence is removed, and
after a state load, because the load writes group 0 only. seq_cp gives the destination the value
of the source, because the two sequences then share the cell and its groups.

Who this changes

llama_memory_seq_rm returns false in place of a wrong state. tools/server already takes that
path: server-context.cpp compares n_rollback against llama_n_rs_seq and restores a checkpoint
when the rollback is too deep, and a false return reaches the same recovery. The app does not
reach the limit: spec_step submits 1 + n_draft tokens and rolls back at most n_draft of them,
thus r is always less than T.

State of the verification

The host program above ran on the CPU backend with the 2B Q4_0 file, with n_rs_seq 3 and 8.
The Android build of the pinned container compiles the change. The device did not run it: the
patch changes no kernel and no graph, thus no speed number moves.

The same bound in the DeepSeek V4 cache

llama_kv_cache_dsv4 keeps n_rs_seq snapshot planes of the compressed state. The plan of a ubatch
writes plane d from the first n - d tokens of the sequence (n is its token count in the ubatch),
and a plane d > n from none of them: that plane holds the state at the start of the ubatch, which
belongs to a different position. A check of the rollback against n_rs_seq alone lets a rollback of
3 after three 1-token ubatches read a plane of a different position, with no error.

The cache keeps rs_depth for each sequence. The memory context sets it in apply(), one time for
each ubatch, to the token count of the sequence less one, at most n_rs_seq. seq_rm takes it as the
limit. The limit is n - 1 and not n (plane n is also correct): the recurrent memory cannot roll
back a full ubatch, and the two memories keep one rule for the callers. The value goes to zero when
the compressed state of the sequence is cleared, after seq_cp (the copy moves the current state
only) and after a state load. The app does not use this cache.

The test: ctest test-recurrent-state-rollback-dsv4, and the qwen35, nemotron-h and kimi-k3 entries.

The rule in test-recurrent-state-rollback

The test asserts that seq_rm refuses a rollback that goes deeper than the tokens of the last ubatch
of the sequence, at the two places where it rolls back past them: test_rollback after three decodes
of one token each, and test_multi_seq_split_replay for a tail ubatch of 3 tokens. The slot groups
that such a rollback reads hold the states of an earlier ubatch. Without the depth bound the test
passes only because its replay decodes the same tokens as that earlier ubatch, thus the old groups
hold the correct states by chance.

Then test_rollback clears the sequence, decodes the prompt again as one ubatch and rolls back inside
it, and test_multi_seq_split_replay decodes the sequence again with a last ubatch that starts one
token before the rollback point. The partial checkpoint and the split replay then cover a pending
rollback (rs_idx != 0), as the test intends. The reference context of the split replay keeps its one
prefill ubatch.

llama_context_ptr owners free the three contexts of test_rollback on each return path, and the init
failure of the multi-sequence part calls its cleanup. Explicit frees at the end of a function leak
the contexts on each failed check, thus LeakSanitizer reports them on top of the failure and the
report hides the failure.

